 { In my view, there is no phase of chess that rewards schematic thinking more heavily than the endgame. But what exactly is schematic thinking? To understand this, we must define what a 'schema' is. A schema is 'a mental framework that represents typical patterns of piece arrangement and potential moves'. Let's break this definition down into its two key parts: (1) 'piece arrangement': a static way of arranging, or configuring, the pieces. This could also be thought of as patterns of piece placement. (2) 'potential moves': This involves also understanding the typical strategies and sequences of moves that can be executed from any given pattern or configuration. If you are anything like me, it is difficult to make sense of a concept just through words alone. Let's connect these definitions to our first example now. In the following endgame, Black is up a pawn and is searching for a path to victory. However, there is no clear forcing line to calculate. This is where schematic thinking comes into play. Black is searching for two things in order to make progress: the right piece arrangement, as well as the potential moves surrounding that arrangement. } 1... { -KEY- } Nh8 $1 { Black makes an unusual looking move, driving the knight to the corner of the board! But it will only stay there for one move, as Black has a specific maneuver in mind. } 2. Kg1 { White sits and waits, as they have no good active plan available. } Ng6 $1 3. Kh2 Nf4 $19 { The knight reaches its destination. This is the ideal piece setup, or schema, that Black was aiming for. Why is this an ideal arrangement? The reason is two-fold. Firstly, from here the knight protects Black's most vulnerable pawns, the light-squared pawns on e6 and h5. Secondly, the knight covers the h3 square which stops the White king from targeting h4. In this example, the Black player used schematic thinking to a) identify the ideal piece (and pawn) configuration , and to b) find the specific moves to reach that configuration. Lastly, schematic thinking also includes identifying a path to making progress once the initial arrangement has been completed. In this case, that would involve bringing the king to the queenside and marching the a- and b-pawns forward. Let's see how the game might play out from here. } 4. Bc4 Ke7 5. Kg1 Kd6 6. Kf2 Kc5 { The next step of the plan has been completed. } 7. Bb3 b5 $1 { Black begins to push the pawns forward in order to make further progress with the king. } 8. Kf1 a5 9. a3 { White recognises that they must stop Black's plan. } ( { If } 9. Kf2 { then after } a4 $1 10. Bd1 Kc4 { the c3 pawn will fall on the next move. } ) 
a4 10. Ba2 { Black has made a lot of progress, but now more concrete action is required to break down the last of White's defenses. } h3 $1 11. gxh3 h4 { Black is in no rush to grab the pawn, they will take it on the next move with check. } 12. Kg1 Nxh3+ 13. Kh2 Nf4 14. Kh1 { The king is forced to wait near the h-pawn. } Ne2 $1 { The perfect moment to strike at the undefended c3 pawn. } 15. Bxe6 Nxc3 16. Kh2 Nb1 $19 { Black will pick up the a3 pawn and will soon make a queen. In this endgame, the path to victory was long, and very few chess players could have calculated it to the end from the starting position. Instead, Black used schematic thinking to focus on finding the best piece configuration possible from the starting position. In this case, that meant placing the knight on f4. The schema included the awareness that once this was achieved Black would be able to move the king forward and, in connection with the pawns, make a decisive breakthrough on the queenside. Next, let's take a look at a simple schema from a theoretical rook endgame position. } 
