 { White may not have calculated this position to the end, but knows that with this particular piece configuration the endgame is winning. It is not just that Black is down a pawn, but also that their rook is tied passively to the defense of the h-pawn. From here, White knows that Black's king can never move to the kingside to win the pawn since the king and pawn endgame would be lost. Therefore, in this arrangement (or schema) Black can do nothing besides shuffling their king around. } 1. { -KEY- } Kb4 Kb7 2. Kc5 Kc7 3. Rh6 Kb7 4. c4 $18 { The c6 pawn is about to fall. If Black plays ...Kc7 then White can play Rxc6+, while any other king move allows Kxc6. With a second pawn gained, White can win in many ways. Let's now turn to the next position, where we can see how White was able to apply the knowledge of this schema to force a quick victory. } 
