 { Can you find a sequence for White that forces the schema from the previous position? The key elements in that arrangement were that both rooks were sitting on the h-file, with Black's rook passively defending the pawn from behind. } 1. { -KEY- } Rd6+ $1 ( 1. Rg7 { This move, and 1.Rg8, also win. However, they involve the use of different arrangements, or schemas. } ) 
Kc7 2. Rd4 $1 Rxc5 3. Rh4 $1 { Correct! White has now established the rook on the h-file. Black has no choice but to maneuver their own rook to the h8 square to prevent the pawn from queening. } Rb5+ 4. Ka3 Rb8 5. h7 Rh8 6. Kxa4 Kb6 7. Kb4 $18 { We have reached the same position as in the previous example. Let's take a look at a real-world example next. } 
