 { This position was reached in the game Corzo,J - Capablanca,J (1901). } 43. { -KEY- } b5 $1 { A strong and safe positional move, killing the activity of the Black bishop and removing potential targets from its sight. Even better, White can devise a winning plan from here. } ( { Unfortunately the White player not only failed to find the winning plan but also walked into a losing configuration! } 43. Nb2 $2 Kg3 { The king enters, and suddenly the g2-f3-e4 pawn chain cannot be saved. } 44. Kf1 h4 45. Na4 cxb4 46. axb4 h3 $1 47. gxh3 Kxf3 $19 { Capablanca went on to win the game some twenty or so moves later. } ) 
Kg3 44. Kf1 h4 { Black has nothing better to try than to play for ...h3. } 45. Nc1 $1 { The knight is moving to e2 with check, which means that the f3 pawn will be safe. } h3 46. Ne2+ Kh4 47. gxh3 Kxh3 { This schema is very safe for White. Even if an experienced player only saw this variation when playing 43.b5, they would be likely to go for it since they know that there is zero risk involved and they may be able to find a winning plan once the position appears on the board. If you wish, you can pause here and see if you can find a winning piece arrangement before reading on. } 48. Nc1 $1 { White decides that his knight is not needed to guard the g3 square. He has devised the following winning arrangement in his mind: (1) My king will go to e2 to defend my pawn on f3. (2) My knight will go to b3, to support the advance of my pawn - first to a5 and then to a6. (3) From there, Black's bishop will be tied down to the a5-d8 diagonal since otherwise I will be able to play b5-b6, sacrificing a pawn and promoting my a-pawn. (4) If Black does nothing, I will then reroute my knight from b3 to d5. Once it is there, it will support the b6 square, allowing me to play b5-b6 and force promotion. At this point the player is very happy to have found a clear path to victory. All that is left is checking to see if there are any hidden resources left for Black against this plan. Here is what his thought process might look like: 'Hmm, in my calculations everything looks good, but I notice that I'm assuming that the Black king will stay motionless on g3. But what if the Black king were to move back to stop the pawn sacrifice on b6?' It turns out that White also has several winning configurations against this defensive plan. I'll leave this path without analysis, so any readers who wish to take up the challenge can search for a winning plan against 49...Kh4. Now, let's take a look at how play might unfold. } Kg3 49. Ke2 { Step 1: the King goes to e2 to defend the f3 pawn. } Ba5 ( 49... Kh4 { Black might also spot the opponent's winning plan and decide to run back with his king to the queenside. If you'd like to test yourself, you can play around with this position until you are confident that you have found a winning plan for White here. } ) 
50. Nb3 Bb6 51. a4 Bd8 52. a5 Bc7 53. a6 { Step 2: The pawn, supported by the knight from b3, has reached the a6 square. } Bb6 54. Nd2 Bc7 55. Nb1 Bb6 56. Nc3 Bd8 57. Nd5 { Step 3: The knight lands on d5, preparing to support the pawn sacrifice on b6. } Kh4 58. b6 $1 { Finally White makes the decisive breakthrough. } Bxb6 59. Nxb6 axb6 60. a7 { White wins. Capablanca may have been lucky to not be on the losing side of this endgame. However, this was a rare situation for him, as he was miles ahead of the majority of his contemporaries when it came to endgame technique. And a big part of this was his ability to spot winning schemas from afar, even before his rivals sensed the danger. Let's check out an example of this now. } 
