 { This position was reached in the game Janowski,D - Capablanca,J (1916) . You might recall that this position was featured in a much earlier chapter. } 20... { -KEY- } Kf7 { A crafty move by Capablanca! He waits patiently, hoping to invite a mistake from the opponent. } 21. e5 $2 { White takes the bait. At first sight it looks good to grab space. Unfortunately for Janowski, he did not peer as deeply into future possibilities as his opponent. } ( 21. exf5 $1 { In the chapter on 'Outposts' we mentioned that either this move or 21.exd5 was best, with the idea being that after } exf5 22. f4 $1 b5 23. Nf3 $11 { the knight is able to reach the power outpost on e5, offering White enough piece activity that the position would have remained balanced. } ) 
Be7 $1 { We see the logic applied by Capablanca with the move 20...Kf7. He wanted to vacate the e7 square for his bishop. } 22. f4 $2 { White continues grabbing space, without realising that it plays directly into Black's long-term plans. Let's consider what options are available to Black to try to push for the full point. (1) The pawn break with ...b5-b4. (2) The pawn break with ...g5, followed either by ...g4 or ...gxf4. Capablanca decides to combine these two, starting by pushing the b-pawn. } b5 { Now that Black has a plan in mind, the question becomes what is the best schema to aim for. Let's take a look at what Capablanca chooses: } 23. Kf2 Ra4 $1 ( 23... Rab8 $6 { Other players may have gone for the same broad plan (to break on b4) but chosen an inferior piece arrangement (schema). This can quickly lead to losing any advantage. } 24. Ke3 b4 $6 25. axb4 Bxb4 26. Ra7 $14 { The poor configuration allows White to enter along the seventh rank, leaving them with an equal or even slightly better position. } ) 
24. Ke3 Rca8 $1 { This is the piece arrangement that Capablanca goes for in the end. What makes this a great configuration is that now the threatened pawn break would win material. } 25. Rab1 { White is forced to go passive. } h6 $1 { Black knows that the ...b4 break is not going away any time soon, so he waits for a better moment before playing the move. This is what chess players mean when they say that sometimes 'the threat is stronger than the execution'. Now White has to constantly worry about lines opening on either flank. } 26. Nf3 g5 27. Ne1 Rg8 $1 ( 27... b4 $6 { Breaking immediately would allow White to solve their problems after } 28. axb4 Bxb4 29. b3 $11 { A lot of material will soon be exchanged on the queenside, with an equal position. } ) 
28. Kf3 gxf4 29. gxf4 Raa8 30. Ng2 Rg4 $1 { Capablanca searches for the most powerful configuration along the open g-file. } 31. Rg1 $6 { In an increasingly uncomfortable position, White self-pins along the open file. } ( 31. Ne3 { If White had chased away the rook from g4, this is likely the schema that Capablanca had in mind: } Rh4 $1 32. Rh1 Rh3 $1 33. Ke2 Be8 $1 34. Kf2 Bh4 35. Ke2 Kg7 $19 { White is helpless against the continued invasion of all of Black's pieces. } ) 
Rag8 $1 32. Be1 b4 $5 { Capablanca now turns his focus to the pawn break from earlier, with a clever idea in mind. } ( 32... Be8 $1 { was even stronger. } ) 
33. axb4 Ba4 $1 { This was what Black had seen. Now the bishop threatens to move to e4. } 34. Ra1 Bc2 35. Bg3 $2 { White misses the final defensive resource. } ( 35. Ra7 $1 { was the last chance to save the game. Incredibly, after } Be4 36. Ke3 $1 Bxg2 { White has just enough counterplay to hold the balance with the move } ( { If } 36. Bg3 Rxg2 { then } 37. Rxg2 Rxg2 38. Bh4 $11 { recovers the missing piece. } ) 
37. b5 $1 { when after } Bh3 38. Rxg4 fxg4 39. Bb4 $1 { Black has to make a draw with the move } g3 { or } Kg6 { . The reason is that if they try to save their extra piece with } Re8 $4 { , then after } 40. Bxe7 Rxe7 41. b6 $18 { the b-pawn will soon become a queen. } ) 
Be4+ 36. Kf2 { Black has positioned all of their pieces ideally. Now all that is left is to make the final breakthrough. } h5 $1 { The threat of ...h4 decides the game. } 37. Ra7 Bxg2 38. Rxg2 h4 39. Bxh4 Rxg2+ 40. Kf3 Rxh2 41. Bxe7 Rh3+ 42. Kf2 Rb3 { The Black rooks will pick off the weak pawns, while there are no strong discovered checks for White. } 43. Bg5+ Kg6 44. Re7 Rxb2+ 45. Kf3 Ra8 46. Rxe6+ Kh7 { White resigned as there is no way to stop the threat of ...Ra3#. In this game we saw that Capablanca's victory was as much about identifying the right plan (pawn breaks with b4 and g5) as it was about identifying the piece configurations that would put the White position under maximum strain. White was able to control the action on the queenside initially, but failed to find the right defense against the invasion of the rooks along the g-file and was quickly overwhelmed. } 
