 { Corzo was one of the strongest players of his time, as evidenced by the fact that he was able to reach a winning position against Capablanca in the 1901 game that we looked at a couple of positions ago. However, at the time schematic thinking was not second nature to top players as it is to Grandmasters today. In the current position, we see that Corzo once again has a position that he should not lose. However, as we shall now see, he once again goes wrong. } 25. { -KEY- } Ke2 $6 { The first sign that White is uncertain about how to hold the slightly inferior endgame. Now Black is able to play the desired pawn break. } ( 25. Rf5 $1 { Black wants to break with ...h5, while the other way of entering the White position is along the d-file. White should have started with this move to stop the pawn break. } Rd6 26. Ke2 $1 { From e2 the king guards all the entry squares. It also monitors the g4 pawn, an important detail in case Black goes for a schema involving placing his rook on g8 to try to target the pawn at the right moment. } c6 27. Rh5 $1 { If Black sits and waits, then White will shuffle his rook between f5 and h5. } Rd8 28. Rf5 $11 { Black has no easy way to make further progress, since } Rg8 { could even land them in a little bit of practical trouble after } 29. g5 $1 { Had White found this configuration, it is a near-certainty that he would have been able to make a straightforward draw even against an endgame maestro like Capablanca. Let's take a look at what happened in the game itself. } ) 
h5 $1 26. Rf5 $4 { Under pressure, White breaks and gives up the wrong pawn. } ( 26. gxh5 $1 { The capture was still good enough to hold the draw. Let's take a look at the configuration that White could achieve here: } Kxh5 27. Ke3 { White simply waits for the pawn to be captured. } Kxh4 28. Rf5 $1 { The key move. With this piece arrangement the king finds itself permanently locked out of play. In spite of an extra two pawns for Black, he is completely unable to make progress. For example: } Rd6 29. Ke2 Kg4 30. Ke1 $11 { The only square available for the Black rook is d3, but then the pawn on f6 will fall, with more to come. There is no other active plan for Black, so the game is drawn. } ) 
hxg4 27. Kf2 Rc4 28. h5+ ( 28. Ke3 { was a better try, when Black would have had nothing better than to return to c6 with the rook. } Rc6 29. Kf2 Rd6 $1 { The right plan. } 30. Ke2 c5 $19 { Now White is in zugzwang. If he moves the rook from f5 then ...Kh5 will follow, while if he plays } 31. Ke1 { then } Rd3 $1 { will invade. } ) 
Kg7 29. h6+ { Desperation. All of White's kingside pawns will now be captured. } Kxh6 30. Rxf6+ Kg7 ( { White resigned, since after } 30... Kg7 30. Rf5 Rxe4 { will leave Black up three pawns. We see that in this game White's loss was due to a failure to find the right defensive scheme, a skill that is essential both as the attacking and defending side. Next up, we'll close out the section with three positions where you will be tested to find the right scheme both offensively and defensively. Let's take a look! } ) 

