 { This position was reached in an online game between Magnus Carlsen and Daniel Naroditsky. Can you find the strong move that Magnus played in order to achieve the best piece configuration? } 1. { -KEY- } Nc4 $1 { A brilliant move! White achieves several things with this move. Firstly, he covers his main structural weakness (the c2 pawn), secondly he moves his knight from the undefended d6 square to the more stable post on c4. However, the major benefit of this move is that by targeting Black's bishop, Magnus forces Naroditsky to change the structure to benefit him. } Rxa4 { This move is clearly best, since 1...b6 fails to 2.Rba1 as shown in the analysis. } ( 1... b6 $2 { This was Black's choice in the game. It's understandable that Black did not want to change the structure, but here the cure is worse than the disease. } 2. Rba1 $1 { Threatening to play 3.Rxb4 followed by 4.Rxa6. } Rbb5 { This is according to the engine Black's best chance, but after } 3. b4 $1 Rxb4 4. Rxb4 Bxb4 5. Rxa6 b5 6. Ne5 $1 { White is dominating here, since White's knight on the e5 square is on a 'pseudo-outpost'. What this means is that Black cannot push back the knight with ...f6 since it would allow the White knight to move forward to the new outpost on g6. } Rxc2 $2 { The attempt to gain the pawn loses instantly. } 7. Nc6 $18 { A clever tactic. White has the dual threats of 8.Nxb4 and 8.Ra8#, so Black is losing material. } ) 
2. bxa4 { The creation of a semi-open b-file and the increased control over the b5 square yields a couple of huge benefits for White: the first is that the rook on b1 becomes active, and the second is that the knight on c4 is almost as stable as if it were on a true outpost. } b6 { Black has no other move if they wish to avoid material losses. } 3. Nxb6 $1 { White transitions from one favorable piece arrangement to another, even more favorable one. } ( 3. Ke4 $6 { looks winning at first glance, but after } f5 $1 { Black breaks up the kingside pawns, and after } 4. gxf5 Rxf5 $11 { The pressure against f4 and h5 allows Black to hold the balance. } ) 
Rxc2 4. Nc4 $1 { With the b6 and c2 pawns exchanged, the White rook can now invade along the b-file. With perfect play Black may be able to hold a draw from here, but in practice it is extremely difficult since the White pieces are much more active while Black's a6 pawn and king safety are constant concerns. In this game we saw how Magnus used tactics to force a change in the structure. This change led to him having stronger pieces, allowing him to win when Black failed to find the toughest defense. } 
