 ( { I reached this position in the game Astaneh Lopez, A - Szabo, K (2008). There, I let the win escape by wrongly trading off rooks with } 56. Rd8 $2 { What move should I have played to force a win? } ) 
56. { -KEY- } Re3 $1 { Correct! This move stops both 56...Be4 and 56...Bd7. Crucially, it also keeps the b3 pawn protected. Moves that allow a piece to be useful in several ways are great candidates to consider when searching for the best schema. } ( 56. Rd8 $2 { This move was very tempting for me, but unfortunately it gave Black a second life. From here, my opponent defended in instructive fashion: } Rxd8 57. Nxd8 Bc2 $1 58. Kc4 g5 $1 59. fxg5 Kg6 60. Ne6 Bf5 61. Nd4 Bd7 $11 { My Grandmaster opponent finds the exact best schema with the a5 pawn and bishop combining well to slow down White's progress on the queenside. } 62. Kc5 { 62.Kd3 was my choice in the game, but once the king gives up on the plan to attack the a5 pawn it becomes an easy draw for Black. } Kxg5 63. Kb6 Kg4 64. Ne2 Be6 $1 65. Kxa5 Bxb3 66. Kb4 Bxa4 67. Kxa4 Kf3 68. Nf4 Kxg3 69. Nxh5 $11 ) 
 ( 56. Rd4 $6 { is not a bad try as it also stops both 56...Bd7 and 56...Be4. However, it has one major drawback: it does not defend the b3 pawn! } Bc2 $1 57. b4 axb4 58. Rxb4 $16 { White still has good practical chances, though the computer points out that with best play Black should be able to make a draw. } ) 
Kh6 { Black has no way to pose any problems, freeing White to chase down the a5 pawn. } 57. Kb6 g5 58. fxg5+ Kxg5 59. Nxa5 $18 { With two connected passed pawns and an ideal defensive configuration with the rook on e3 and pawn on g3, there is little chance for Black to muddy the waters. In this game, we saw how my major mistake was failing to find the ideal schema with 56.Re3! Had I done so, I would have covered all of Black's threats while defending my vulnerable pawns on b3 and g3. I hope you've enjoyed this section. In the next one, we'll take a look at two concepts that are surprisingly closely related to schematic thinking: paralysis, and fortresses. See you there. } 
